# Line endings are not a style preference in this repository. Git's index
# already stores LF for every text file here, so the only thing `core.autocrlf`
# changes is the working tree - and the working tree is what gets bind-mounted
# into Linux containers. A shell script checked out with CRLF fails there with
#
#     /usr/local/bin/dbt-entrypoint: 1: set: Illegal option -
#
# which names neither the file nor the cause, because `sh` reads `set -eu\r` as
# an unknown option rather than as a line ending. Pinning `eol=lf` makes the
# working tree agree with the index on Windows as well, so the file that is
# edited on this machine is the file that runs in the container.
#
# `text=auto` keeps binary artifacts - the committed Remotion media under
# `motion/` - out of it: git detects them as binary and this rule never applies.
* text=auto eol=lf
